\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The edge-source triple and inner fluctuations}
\date{2026-11-03}

\p{Let #{G} be a finite oriented graph, with vertex set #{V} and edge
set #{E}. Write #{s(e)} and #{h(e)} for the source and head of an
edge. No edge is a loop. Either set may be empty. Let
#{H=\mathbb R^V\oplus\mathbb R^E} carry the counting inner products,
and let #{\delta_v} and #{\delta_e} be the corresponding indicator
functions. Define
##{(d_E\xi)(e)=\xi(h(e))-\xi(s(e)).}
Let #{D} be the self-adjoint operator whose vertex-to-edge block is
#{d_E} and whose edge-to-vertex block is the adjoint #{d_E^{\dagger}}.
A real function #{f} on #{V} acts by multiplication by #{f(v)} on
vertex #{v} and by multiplication by #{f(s(e))} on edge #{e}.}

\p{The operator norm of the commutator is read from the jumps on the
edges that enter each vertex. For every vertex #{v},
##{\bigl|[D,f]\bigr|
=\max_v\Bigl(\sum_{h(e)=v}\bigl(f(h(e))-f(s(e))\bigr)^{2}\Bigr)^{1/2}.}
A sum over an empty set of edges is zero. The maximum of a
nonnegative quantity over the empty set of vertices is zero. These
conventions cover an empty edge set and an empty vertex set. They
do not add a hypothesis that either set is nonempty.
If every vertex has at most one incoming edge, this norm is the
maximum of #{|f(h(e))-f(s(e))|} over the edges. On each connected
component of the underlying undirected graph, the supremum
##{\sup\bigl\{|f(j)-f(i)|:\bigl|[D,f]\bigr|\le 1\bigr\}}
equals the least number of edges joining the two vertices. Between
distinct components that supremum is infinite. A directed cycle has
one incoming edge at each vertex, so opposite vertices of a directed
#{6}-cycle are at distance #{3}.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{\delta_e=f(h(e))-f(s(e))}. The edge component of
#{[D,f]} applied to #{(\xi,\psi)} is #{\delta_e\xi(h(e))}, and the
vertex component is the negative adjoint of that edge map applied
to #{\psi}. The operator norm is therefore the norm of the edge map
#{C\xi(e)=\delta_e\xi(h(e))}. Its square length is
##{\|C\xi\|^{2}=\sum_v\xi(v)^{2}\sum_{h(e)=v}\delta_e^{2}.}
The counting inner product makes the vertex indicators orthonormal, so the second sum is the
eigenvalue at #{v}. If #{V} is empty, both the operator and the
maximum are zero by the convention in the statement. If a vertex
has no incoming edge, its inner sum is zero. Otherwise the largest square root is the displayed
maximum.}

\p{If each vertex has at most one incoming edge, the inner sum is a
single square, and the norm is the largest absolute jump. The
constraint then says that #{|f(h)-f(s)|\le 1} on every edge. The
graph-distance function from #{i} changes by at most #{1} along an
edge, so it is allowed, and its value at #{j} is the graph
distance. Any allowed function changes by at most #{1} along each
edge, so it cannot exceed that distance. If no path joins #{i} to
#{j}, adding a constant on the component of #{j} does not change
the commutator, and the difference is unbounded.}
}

\p{The same norm is stricter where a vertex has two incoming edges.
On the grid whose coordinates each run through #{0,1,2}, orient
each edge in the positive direction of one axis. The distance from
#{(0,0)} to #{(1,1)} is #{1+1/\sqrt{2}}. The Euclidean distance is
#{\sqrt{2}} and the graph distance is #{2}, and the commutator
distance lies strictly between them.}

\proof{
\p{Write #{x=f(1,0)-f(0,0)}, #{y=f(0,1)-f(0,0)},
#{p=f(1,1)-f(1,0)} and #{q=f(1,1)-f(0,1)}. The vertex #{(1,0)} has
only the incoming edge from #{(0,0)}, and #{(0,1)} has only the
incoming edge from #{(0,0)}, so an allowed function satisfies
#{x^{2}\le 1} and #{y^{2}\le 1}. The vertex #{(1,1)} has those two
incoming edges, so #{p^{2}+q^{2}\le 1}. The difference
#{\delta=f(1,1)-f(0,0)} equals both #{x+p} and #{y+q}. Thus
#{(\delta-x)^{2}+(\delta-y)^{2}\le 1} for some #{x,y} in the
interval from #{-1} to #{1}. If #{\delta>1+1/\sqrt{2}}, then both
#{\delta-x} and #{\delta-y} exceed #{1/\sqrt{2}}, and the sum of
squares exceeds #{1}. Hence #{\delta\le 1+1/\sqrt{2}}.}

\p{The bound is attained by #{f(0,0)=0}, #{f(1,0)=f(0,1)=f(2,0)=1},
#{f(0,2)=1}, and the value #{1+1/\sqrt{2}} at the other 4
vertices. The incoming square sums are #{1} at #{(1,0)}, #{(0,1)}
and #{(1,1)}, they are #{1/2} at #{(2,1)} and #{(1,2)}, and they
vanish elsewhere. The norm is #{1}, and the difference of the
endpoint values is #{1+1/\sqrt{2}}. It is larger than #{\sqrt{2}}
because #{1>1/\sqrt{2}}, and it is smaller than #{2} because
#{1/\sqrt{2}<1}.}
}

\p{Inner fluctuations fill the head slots of #{D}. For real functions
#{a} and #{b}, the edge component of #{a[D,b]} applied to a vertex
vector #{\xi} is
##{e\mapsto a(s(e))\bigl(b(h(e))-b(s(e))\bigr)\xi(h(e)).}
Suppose that at most one edge runs from any vertex to any other,
and that the opposite orientation is absent. For an edge #{e} from
#{s} to #{h} and a real scalar #{c}, let #{a} equal #{c} at #{s}
and equal #{0} elsewhere, and let #{b} equal #{1} at #{h} and equal
#{0} elsewhere. The sum #{S=a[D,b]+b[D,a]} is self-adjoint. Its
edge component is #{c\,\xi(h)} on #{e} and zero on every other
edge. Summing one such operator for each edge gives every real
assignment #{(c_e)}. Adding that sum to #{D} replaces the edge
formula by
##{U_e\xi(h(e))-\xi(s(e)),\qquad U_e=1+c_e.}
}

\proof{
\p{The displayed edge formula for #{a[D,b]} is the commutator
calculation already used for the norm: the terms in which #{b} is
read at the source cancel, and multiplication by #{a} is evaluated
at the source. With the two functions just chosen,
#{a(s(e'))(b(h(e'))-b(s(e')))} equals #{c} on the edge from #{s}
to #{h} and equals #{0} on every other edge. The swapped product
#{b(s(e'))(a(h(e'))-a(s(e')))} is nonzero only on an edge from
#{h} to #{s}. No such edge is present.}

\p{It remains to check that #{b[D,a]} is the adjoint of #{a[D,b]}.
The edge block of #{a[D,b]} multiplies the value at #{h} by #{c}
and places it on #{e}. Its adjoint therefore multiplies the value
on #{e} by #{c} and places it at #{h}. The vertex component of a
commutator #{p[D,q]}, evaluated at a vertex #{v}, is
##{-p(v)\sum_{h(e')=v}\bigl(q(h(e'))-q(s(e'))\bigr)\psi(e').}
For #{p=b} and #{q=a}, the only vertex where #{b} is nonzero is
#{h}. The only incoming edge there whose source carries a nonzero
value of #{a} is #{e}, and on that edge
#{a(h)-a(s)=-c}. The leading minus sign turns that term into
#{c\,\psi(e)} at #{h}, and the component is #{0} at every other
vertex. The two blocks agree. For the other pair, the vertex
component of #{a[D,b]} is supported at #{s}, and no edge entering
#{s} starts at #{h}, so that component is #{0}. The edge component
of #{b[D,a]} is #{0} because the reverse edge is absent. Hence
#{S} is self-adjoint and its edge coefficient on #{e} is #{c}.}
}

\p{The coefficients #{U_e} constructed above are real. When
#{U_e\ne 0},
##{U_e\xi(h(e))-\xi(s(e))
=U_e\bigl(\xi(h(e))-U_e^{-1}\xi(s(e))\bigr).}
The second factor is the [covariant difference](fdip-006D) of the
scalar connection #{U_e^{-1}}. The two edge maps have the same
kernel. This construction does not produce a matrix representation
of a nonabelian group.}

\p{If every #{U_e} equals #{\pm 1}, multiplication by #{U_e} is an
orthogonal map #{W} of the edges, and #{W^{-1}=W}. Let #{\extd_1} be
the covariant difference and set #{\extd_2=W\extd_1}. Then
#{\extd_2^{\dagger}=\extd_1^{\dagger}W}. Conjugating the off-diagonal
operator of #{\extd_2} by the orthogonal map that is the identity on
vertices and #{W} on edges replaces the edge block #{\extd_2} by
#{W\extd_2=\extd_1} and replaces #{\extd_2^{\dagger}} by #{\extd_1^{\dagger}}.
The two self-adjoint operators are orthogonally equivalent.}
